    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to consider the arrangements of the groups of books as a single unit. Here's the step-by-step reasoning:\n\n1. **Treat the groups of math books and English books as single units:**\n   - We have two groups: one for the math books and one for the English books.\n   - The number of ways to arrange these two groups on the shelf is \\(2!\\) (2 factorial), which is \\(2 \\times 1 = 2\\).\n\n2. **Arrange the books within each group:**\n   - There are 3 math books, and they can be arranged among themselves in \\(3!\\) (3 factorial) ways. This is calculated as:\n     \\[\n     3! = 3 \\times 2 \\times 1 = 6\n     \\]\n   - There are 5 English books, and they can be arranged among themselves in \\(5!\\) (5 factorial) ways. This is calculated as:\n     \\[\n     5! = 5 \\times 4 \\times 3 \\times 2 \\times 1 = 120\n     \\]\n\n3. **Combine the arrangements:**\n   - The total number of ways to arrange the books is the product of the number of ways to arrange the groups and the number of ways to arrange the books within each group.\n   - Therefore, the total number of arrangements is:\n     \\[\n     2! \\times 3! \\times 5! = 2 \\times 6 \\times 120 = 1440\n     \\]\n\nThus, the number of ways to arrange the 3 math books and 5 English books on a shelf, with all math books together and all English books together, is \\(\\boxed{1440}\\).",
