    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To solve the problem of arranging 3 math books and 5 English books on a shelf while ensuring that all the math books stay together and all the English books stay together, we can follow these steps:\n\n1. **Treat the math books as a single unit and the English books as a single unit:**\n   - There are 3 math books, which we can treat as one unit. Let's call this unit \"M\".\n   - There are 5 English books, which we can treat as another unit. Let's call this unit \"E\".\n   - Together, these two units can be thought of as 2 items to arrange.\n\n2. **Arrange the 2 units on the shelf:**\n   - The number of ways to arrange 2 items is given by the factorial of 2, which is \\(2!\\).\n   \\[\n   2! = 2 \\times 1 = 2\n   \\]\n   So, there are 2 ways to arrange the units \"M\" and \"E\" on the shelf.\n\n3. **Arrange the books within each unit:**\n   - There are 3 math books within the \"M\" unit.\n   \\[\n   3! = 3 \\times 2 \\times 1 = 6\n   \\]\n   So, there are 6 ways to arrange the 3 math books.\n   - There are 5 English books within the \"E\" unit.\n   \\[\n   5! = 5 \\times 4 \\times 3 \\times 2 \\times 1 = 120\n   \\]\n   So, there are 120 ways to arrange the 5 English books.\n\n4. **Calculate the total number of arrangements:**\n   - The total number of ways to arrange the 3 math books and 5 English books with their units fixed is the product of the number of ways to arrange the units and the number of ways to arrange the books within each unit.\n   \\[\n   2! \\times 3! \\times 5! = 2 \\times 6 \\times 120 = 1440\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the total number of ways to arrange the 3 math books and 5 English books on the shelf, with all math books together and all English books together, is \\(\\boxed{1440}\\).",
