    {
      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To solve the problem of arranging 3 math books and 5 English books on a shelf, where all math books must stay together and all English books must stay together, we can follow these steps:\n\n1. **Treat the math books as a single unit and the English books as a single unit:**\n   - Since the math books must stay together, we can think of the 3 math books as forming one \"block\" or single unit.\n   - Similarly, since the English books must stay together, we can think of the 5 English books as forming another \"block\" or single unit.\n\n   This means we have 2 units to arrange: one unit consisting of the 3 math books and one unit consisting of the 5 English books.\n\n2. **Arrange the 2 units on the shelf:**\n   - The number of ways to arrange 2 distinct units on a shelf is the number of permutations of 2 items, which is \\(2!\\) (2 factorial).\n   \\[\n   2! = 2 \\times 1 = 2\n   \\]\n\n3. **Arrange the books within each unit:**\n   - Within the unit of 3 math books, there are \\(3!\\) (3 factorial) ways to arrange the books.\n   \\[\n   3! = 3 \\times 2 \\times 1 = 6\n   \\]\n   - Within the unit of 5 English books, there are \\(5!\\) (5 factorial) ways to arrange the books.\n   \\[\n   5! = 5 \\times 4 \\times 3 \\times 2 \\times 1 = 120\n   \\]\n\n4. **Calculate the total number of arrangements:**\n   - The total number of ways to arrange the 2 units and the books within each unit is the product of the number of ways to arrange the units and the number of ways to arrange the books within each unit.\n   \\[\n   \\text{Total arrangements} = 2! \\times 3! \\times 5!\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\text{Total arrangements} = 2 \\times 6 \\times 120\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\text{Total arrangements} = 1440\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the number of ways to put 3 math books and 5 English books on a shelf, with all math books together and all English books together, is \\(\\boxed{1440}\\).",
